{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1790339400000-architect-dao-stream-serve-q1-q7-mob-consensus.orchestrator.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-09-25 12:36:00 GMT\n     2\tCreated-Local: 2026-09-25 19:36:00 +0700\n     3\tCoding-Agent: interactive\n     4\tSession-ID: not-applicable (interactive seat)\n     5\t\n     6\t# dao.stream.serve Q1-Q7 mob consensus (orchestrator's mechanical summary)\n     7\t\n     8\tOwner instruction (quote): \"have codex and GLM-5.3 mob on Q1-Q7 to reach a concenus\"\n     9\tParticipants: codex gpt-6-sol (thread 01a0d870-7702-7890-bb87-299065a201b8),\n    10\tGLM-5.3 (session ce9476ea-84c0-4d54-aef5-4c38a20bb22f). Six rounds\n    11\t(r1-r6 files under collab/1790337700000..1790339200000). Advice to the\n    12\towner only; nothing has been applied to any spec.\n    13\t\n    14\t| Q | Consensus position | vs Fable's recommendation |\n    15\t|---|---|---|\n    16\t| Q1 | Keep the event-medium design; amend UCF 7.4.3 so a served :put resumes on the observed source outcome; an append-unknown leaves the wait undischarged | same, plus append-unknown |\n    17\t| Q2 | Defer held reads from v1; keep 3.7 additive | differs (Fable: include) |\n    18\t| Q3 | Opaque composition-assigned peer ids in trusted v1; duplicate :serve/register defined as relay policy; self-certifying later; public relay needs channel auth | same direction, plus duplicate rule and tripwire |\n    19\t| Q4 | Relay admission is composition-supplied policy, open only in trusted deployments; peer/link lifecycle and caps required; postage a future policy | differs (Fable: accept any) |\n    20\t| Q5 | Accept OD-2 and OD-3 (a)+(2); accept OD-1 with the write fallback corrected to \"effect unknown; no automatic retry\"; amend two contract definitions (blocked, anchors) plus a serve 4 declaration | differs (Fable: accept all as drafted) |\n    21\t| Q6 | Lease-governed via dao.lease; composition retirement is an explicitly weaker pre-grant stopgap; renewals count only on observed source ok; UCF migration acceptance still needs adequate lifetime | same direction, phased |\n    22\t| Q7 | dao.stream.serve; rename dao.stream.serving to a copy-serving name; fix ws prose | same |\n    23\t\n    24\t## Final draft contract amendments (codex r6, GLM's three edits applied)\n    25\t\n    26\tReading, blocked: \"No observation is available through this handle at this\n    27\tcursor now; the cursor does not advance, and the reader may retry later. A\n    28\thandle declaring deferred remote observation may return blocked while\n    29\tfetching even if the source already holds a value or has evicted that\n    30\tposition. If a source observation completes, its outcome is supplied.\"\n    31\t\n    32\tCursors, anchors: \"A handle declaring deferred remote observation may\n    33\treturn a source-minted anchor from its last completed source observation.\n    34\tIts :newest may precede the source's current tail but must never follow it;\n    35\tits :oldest may precede the currently retained head, in which case a\n    36\tsubsequent next reports the source's gap.\"\n    37\t\n    38\tAlso settled: gap is only ever relayed, never synthesized; end is relayed or\n    39\tlocal (attachment exhausted), per dao.stream.md:603.\n    40\t\n    41\t## Doc edits this implies (none made)\n    42\tUCF 7.4.3 (put resume, single :dao.stream.serve/v1 cursor profile); dao.stream.md\n    43\t(OD-1 corrected, OD-2, OD-3, the two definitions above); dao.stream.ws.md\n    44\t18-26 prose and outbound-serving prose; dao.stream.serving rename;\n    45\tdao.lease.md 286-289; daostream-udp-design.md marked superseded.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md | sed -n '68,82p;112,130p;153,165p;220,233p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md | sed -n '68,82p;112,130p;153,165p;220,233p'\"","aggregated_output":"    68\t## The Mechanisms\n    69\t\n    70\t### 1. Self-certifying identities (secure + decentralized)\n    71\t\n    72\tThe kickoff hash *is* the name. A stream announces its signed head into the DHT under\n    73\tits identity hash; resolution is one lookup plus local signature verification against\n    74\tthe key the kickoff commits to. No authority exists because none is needed — the name\n    75\tproves its own binding (git commit hashes, IPFS CIDs). This is the foundation layer:\n    76\tevery other mechanism resolves *to* these names. Its defect is that a hash is not a\n    77\tname a human can hold.\n    78\t\n    79\tA directory entry binds an *identity*, not a state, so it never goes stale when a\n    80\tstream's head moves — directory churn stays at true membership churn.\n    81\t\n    82\t### 2. Petname webs (memorable + decentralized)\n   112\tread-side transitive closure of announce/follow datoms from wherever a reader starts.\n   113\tAnnouncements form a grow-set CRDT (datoms merge by union — convergent with no\n   114\tcoordination, by construction). Reach is bounded by the social graph, which doubles as\n   115\tspam defense: an unfollowed announcer is not in anyone's closure. This is what can\n   116\tproduce a reader's explicit source pool: discovery becomes a query over single-writer\n   117\tfacts rather than mutation of a privileged registry.\n   118\t\n   119\t### 4. Rendezvous topics (discovery by interest)\n   120\t\n   121\tFor \"who has streams about X\" without privileged indexers: hash the topic (an\n   122\tattribute, a schema name) to a DHT key; providers deposit small self-signed provider\n   123\trecords at the responsible neighborhood; seekers look up the topic key and verify.\n   124\t(IPFS provider records, gossipsub topics.) The neighborhood stores but never authors.\n   125\tThis is also how you *find indexer agents* without anyone anointing them.\n   126\t\n   127\t### 5. Consensus namespaces (globally unique + memorable — bought with consensus)\n   128\t\n   129\tNamecoin/ENS-shaped first-writer-wins claims ordered by a consensus log. Honest\n   130\tassessment: the expensive corner of the triangle, importing the same\n   153\t- **Paying to be seen** — the speaker pays for reach. That is advertising,\n   154\t  definitionally. **Rejected.** It ranks streams by willingness-to-pay (i.e., by\n   155\t  capitalization, not relevance), and it contradicts the project's own pay-for-truth\n   156\t  thesis (`agent-web.md`): pay-for-truth flows money *toward* the party being attended\n   157\t  to, at the attendee's request; paid reach flows money *from* the party demanding\n   158\t  attention. Opposite signs. It is also born Goodharted (below).\n   159\t- **Paying to deposit** — a tiny flat cost to announce (Hashcash, postage).\n   160\t  **Accepted.** This prices spam without ranking by wealth. Note it needs a *cost*,\n   161\t  not a *market*: proof-of-work or burned stamps suffice; no exchange, no price\n   162\t  discovery, no ledger.\n   163\t- **Paying for discovery service** — buying an indexer's query answers.\n   164\t  **Accepted.** That is pay-for-truth applied to discovery, already the doctrine.\n   165\t\n   220\t## Dependencies and Open Questions\n   221\t\n   222\tEverything above rests on prerequisites already tracked elsewhere:\n   223\t\n   224\t1. **Signed heads / kickoff-hash identity** (a stream-layer prerequisite, not\n   225\t   mutable DaoJing state; see `dao.jing.dht.md`) — mechanism (1) *is* this\n   226\t   discipline; (2)–(4) resolve to it.\n   227\t2. **Source-scoped query inputs** (`dao.space.query.md`; `datom.md`, d5:\n   228\t   SOURCE SCOPE) — sources stay separate database inputs and equal stream-local\n   229\t   ids unify only when the query deliberately joins them; prerequisite for\n   230\t   folding strangers' directories.\n   231\t3. **Postage design** — what a deposit costs and who verifies it (proof-of-work vs.\n   232\t   stamps) is unspecified.\n   233\t4. **Pool liveness** — explicit source pools need a refresh convention; the stigmergic\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '69,87p;100,116p;202,243p;257,309p;387,453p;473,553p;558,579p;654,705p;921,944p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '69,87p;100,116p;202,243p;257,309p;387,453p;473,553p;558,579p;654,705p;921,944p'\"","aggregated_output":"    69\t## Invariants\n    70\t\n    71\t- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.\n    72\t- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n    73\t- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n    74\t- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n    75\t- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.\n    76\t- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase\n    77\t  retained history.\n    78\t- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n    79\t  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n    80\t- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n    81\t- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is\n    82\t  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.\n    83\t- No hidden global state. DaoStream keeps no registry of streams, handles, or\n    84\t  observers, and no operation consults ambient state.\n    85\t- A logical stream has an identity that is plain data, the same through every\n    86\t  handle on it, stable across attachment lifecycles and across serialization.\n    87\t  Projecting that identity never fails.\n   100\t- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n   101\t  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n   102\t  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n   103\t  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n   104\t  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n   105\t  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n   106\t- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n   107\t  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n   108\t  addition to it.\n   109\t- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n   110\t  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n   111\t  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n   112\t  added without breaking existing consumers.\n   113\t- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of\n   114\t  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an\n   115\t  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\n   116\t  malformed input, and transport defects are all outcomes, never exceptions\n   202\t## Creation and Attachment\n   203\t\n   204\tCreation brings a new logical stream into existence. Attachment joins an\n   205\texisting one. They are separate operations with separate inputs.\n   206\t\n   207\t- `create!` consumes a creation specification and returns a handle on a\n   208\t  new logical stream. `descriptor` on that handle returns `{ok}` with both\n   209\t  `:dao.stream/descriptor`, the transport-specific reachability value, and\n   210\t  `:dao.stream/identity`, the transport-independent logical-stream identity.\n   211\t  The descriptor carries the same identity internally; the sibling projection\n   212\t  and its value inside `:dao.stream/descriptor` must be structurally equal.\n   213\t  Both projections are total and outlive any attachment, so `descriptor` has\n   214\t  no failure outcome.\n   215\t- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on an\n   216\t  attachment to the **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching\n   217\t  never creates.\n   218\t- A creation specification says how to build a stream. A descriptor only\n   219\t  names one — never executable code, never a construction program, never a\n   220\t  request to load a plugin.\n   221\t\n   222\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams — up to a serialization\n   223\tboundary. An in-memory stream whose admission declaration permits host\n   224\treferences can carry a handle intact. A dynamically delivered handle travels\n   225\tas composition data together with its declared surface, for example\n   226\t`{:dao.stream/handle h :dao.stream/surface #{:reader :writer}}`; the receiving\n   227\tinterpreter uses only that declared surface, and attaching to what it already\n   228\tholds is meaningless. A composition may omit the wrapper only when the\n   229\treceiver's fixed contract already declares the surface. But a handle does not\n   230\tsurvive encoding, and any\n   231\ttransport that encodes its elements — a file as much as a network socket —\n   232\tis a serialization boundary. Descriptors exist for crossing those\n   233\tboundaries, where the live value cannot travel and a name must. (What a\n   234\ttransport can carry is transport-owned: handing an encoding transport a\n   235\tvalue it cannot encode is reported as data like any other outcome.)\n   236\t`attach!` therefore consumes only descriptors, and\n   237\tresolves them against the transport's inherent operational state — the\n   238\tstreams a network endpoint serves because serving them is what it is, the\n   239\tfiles a filesystem holds. There is no stream table in this contract: no\n   240\tregistry maps descriptors to live handles, so a descriptor arriving\n   241\tsomewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other\n   242\thost, or on its own host absent a directory some composition chose to keep —\n   243\thonestly resolves to `:dao.stream/not-found`.\n   257\tThe **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object\n   258\treference. It is reachability data, not the logical-stream identity itself.\n   259\tIts properties are settled:\n   260\t\n   261\t- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch\n   262\t  key.\n   263\t- It carries the logical-stream identity under the contract-owned\n   264\t  `:dao.stream/identity` key. Different descriptors may reach\n   265\t  the same logical stream through different endpoints; their reachability data\n   266\t  may differ, but their logical-stream identity is structurally equal. The same\n   267\t  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.\n   268\t- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is\n   269\t  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is\n   270\t  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns\n   271\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.\n   272\t- A transport whose attachments are distinguishable assigns each attachment\n   273\t  an opaque, structurally serializable value, unique among the attachments\n   274\t  that transport can tell apart. Every attachment handle the transport mints\n   275\t  has such an identity, whether the handle is returned by `attach!` or\n   276\t  minted from a host connection accepted without an `attach!` call. An\n   277\t  `attach!` success map carries the value under `:dao.stream/attachment`.\n   278\t  When no `attach!` call produces the handle, the transport specification\n   279\t  names the deposited event or other stream-native mechanism by which the\n   280\t  composition receives the identity. Wherever an answer or event for that\n   281\t  attachment is displaced onto another channel, the same value correlates it\n   282\t  there. The key and correlation rule are the contract's; minting,\n   283\t  representation, and the server-side delivery mechanism are the\n   284\t  transport's. Transports that cannot distinguish attachments omit it.\n   285\t- It carries no authorization. Nothing in this contract gates who may attach:\n   286\t  a descriptor is a name, and a host that can reach what it names attaches.\n   287\t  Whether attachment should be gated at all, and by what, is a question this\n   288\t  contract does not answer and does not reserve room for — an outcome or key\n   289\t  held open for an undesigned mechanism constrains that mechanism's design\n   290\t  from a document with no business specifying it. Should gating arrive, it\n   291\t  arrives as an addition, which the open-map rule and this section's own\n   292\t  transport-owned keys already permit.\n   293\t\n   294\tFor the time being, the operative guarantee is the round trip: `descriptor`\n   295\ton a live handle returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/descriptor`,\n   296\ta value that can be sent on a stream and reconstructed on the other end, and\n   297\t`:dao.stream/identity`, equal through every handle and descriptor for the\n   298\tlogical stream,\n   299\twhere `attach!` on the reconstructed value attaches to the stream it names —\n   300\tprovided the receiving host can reach what it names at all (see Creation and\n   301\tAttachment on reachability), and allowing that a transport which cannot know\n   302\tthat at call time reports it later as data.\n   303\t\n   304\tBoth envelopes are plain data through and through: every value must survive\n   305\tthe host serialization codec structurally unchanged, so equality is\n   306\tstructural, and no key holds a function, a host object, a live handle, or\n   307\tidentity smuggled in metadata. Like every DaoStream map, envelopes are open —\n   308\ta consumer ignores qualified keys it does not understand.\n   309\t\n   387\t## Surfaces\n   388\t\n   389\tA handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can\n   390\thonor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**\n   391\t(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and\n   392\tRetention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.\n   393\t\n   394\t`descriptor` belongs to no surface: every handle answers it with both\n   395\treachability and logical-stream identity, because both outlive any attachment.\n   396\tAn operation a handle does not\n   397\tdeclare is not part of that handle at all: there is nothing to call, so there\n   398\tis no operation for this contract to give an outcome. What a host does when\n   399\tasked for a method that is not there is the host's, and no transport may\n   400\tanswer such a call with a DaoStream outcome map — an outcome would imply the\n   401\toperation exists.\n   402\t\n   403\tA transport may also offer operations of its own beside the public surface —\n   404\tanswering a question this contract does not ask, such as how far a cursor\n   405\ttrails the newest value. They are transport-owned, they are not DaoStream\n   406\toperations, and nothing here defines or constrains them. **Code written\n   407\tagainst this contract may not reach for one**: it must work on any transport\n   408\tdeclaring the surfaces it uses, and a transport-owned operation is by\n   409\tdefinition absent from some of them.\n   410\t\n   411\tAn interpreter may still use one, on the same terms as any other thing a\n   412\tcomposition supplies. What it may not do is *find* it — reach through a handle\n   413\tfor an operation the contract never promised, or dispatch on which transport\n   414\tit was given. It receives the operation as an ordinary argument from the\n   415\tcomposition that wired it, exactly as it receives the streams it reads and the\n   416\tpolicies it applies, and it then depends on a function it was handed rather\n   417\tthan on a transport it detected. Such an interpreter is composed for a\n   418\tparticular medium and says so; it is not contract-generic code, and nothing\n   419\tcontract-generic may be built on it.\n   420\t\n   421\tThe reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as\n   422\tone positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\n   423\tre-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that\n   424\tpromise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the\n   425\thost's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary\n   426\t— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a\n   427\ttransport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the\n   428\ttransport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per\n   429\tits declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly\n   430\tdefect. Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated: the contract's\n   431\tpublic surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy. A constructor\n   432\ttakes the deposit destination and its admission declaration as data. This is\n   433\tconfiguration provenance, not runtime introspection. (`dao.stream.ws.md`\n   434\tspecifies one such transport and the admission rule in detail.)\n   435\t\n   436\tA boundary that can no longer deposit must make itself **observably gone**. Its\n   437\tone permitted action is closing the host resource it itself holds; it invokes\n   438\tnothing above itself, retries nothing, and interprets nothing. This is the sole\n   439\taction a boundary adapter takes beyond appending, and it is not a judgement\n   440\tabout any event — it is the same action for every cause. One corner is\n   441\tirreducible: when the deposit that failed was the boundary's only channel, the\n   442\trecord of its own departure has nowhere to land, and the failure is observable\n   443\tonly to whoever holds the deposit result and through subsequent operations on\n   444\tthe handle.\n   445\t\n   446\tA transport also declares **which outcomes it produces**, and for each one it\n   447\texcludes, why. There are three honest reasons: the outcome's precondition is\n   448\timpossible by the transport's nature (an unbounded log never evicts, so never\n   449\treports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer\n   450\tevicts rather than refusing, so pressure surfaces as a `gap` to a cursor that\n   451\tspans the eviction, and never as `full`); or the answer is displaced to another channel, named (a transport\n   452\tthat cannot know a remote answer at call time reports it as data elsewhere). An\n   453\texclusion with no stated reason is an unimplemented outcome, not a declaration.\n   473\t## Cursors\n   474\t\n   475\tA cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   476\t\n   477\t- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\n   478\t  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n   479\t  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained\n   480\t  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended\n   481\t  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport\n   482\t  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional\n   483\t  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\n   484\t  malformed or not honored by this transport yields\n   485\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\n   486\t  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n   487\t  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains\n   488\t  readable. Minting may have to consult the medium — locating the earliest\n   489\t  retained position on a durable log is itself a read — so where a\n   490\t  transport's reads can fail at runtime, minting can too, and `cursor`\n   491\t  yields `:dao.stream/transport-error` for it; a transport whose medium\n   492\t  cannot fail this way excludes the outcome like any other (see Surfaces).\n   493\t  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n   494\t  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n   495\t  `:dao.stream/closed`, and `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n   496\t- A composition that intends to observe events caused by an operation mints\n   497\t  its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n   498\t  after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n   499\t  that can deposit would intentionally skip events deposited in between.\n   500\t- **A freshly minted `:dao.stream/oldest` is a position, not a claim about\n   501\t  completeness.** The anchor names the earliest *retained* position, which on\n   502\t  an evicting transport advances as values are lost. A `gap` is reported to a\n   503\t  cursor that spans an eviction; a cursor minted after one is a valid cursor\n   504\t  onto the surviving suffix and reports nothing, because nothing is wrong with\n   505\t  the cursor. Retained history is not complete history, and no anchor can make\n   506\t  it so. A consumer that requires complete history gets it from the transport's\n   507\t  declared retention (see *Retention and Gaps*), never from an anchor.\n   508\t- A composition on a transport that **can evict**, intending to detect whether\n   509\t  history was lost, mints an **origin cursor** — `:dao.stream/oldest` before\n   510\t  the first append — and keeps it, exactly as a composition that intends to\n   511\t  observe events caused by an operation mints `:dao.stream/newest` before\n   512\t  invoking it. A kept origin cursor converts a silent loss into a reported\n   513\t  `gap`. It cannot be minted afterwards and cannot be reconstructed: a consumer\n   514\t  that arrives later holds no evidence about what preceded it. On a transport\n   515\t  that declares complete retention this is unnecessary — a fresh `:oldest` is\n   516\t  the origin there, however late the consumer arrives.\n   517\t- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as\n   518\t  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never\n   519\t  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A\n   520\t  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.\n   521\t- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the\n   522\t  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter\n   523\t  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives\n   524\t  serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD, like the\n   525\t  descriptor envelope.\n   526\t- A cursor carries the logical-stream identity, not a reachability descriptor.\n   527\t  `cursor-mismatch` compares that identity, so handles reached through distinct\n   528\t  descriptors for the same logical stream accept the same cursor.\n   529\t- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never\n   530\t  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a\n   531\t  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.\n   532\t- Because cursors are immutable values, a reader may retain an old cursor and\n   533\t  re-read retained history, or hand a cursor to another interpreter. What a\n   534\t  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.\n   535\t\n   536\t## Reading\n   537\t\n   538\t`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\n   539\tIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\n   540\tanswer.\n   541\t\n   542\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\n   543\t|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n   544\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n   545\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   546\t| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n   547\t| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n   548\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n   549\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   550\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n   551\t\n   552\tReading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same\n   553\tposition observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the\n   558\t## Writing\n   559\t\n   560\t`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:\n   561\t\n   562\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                                                                                              | Required keys |\n   563\t|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|\n   564\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |\n   565\t| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\n   566\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\n   567\t| `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |\n   568\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append; nothing was appended.                                                                                    | —             |\n   569\t\n   570\tAcceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a\n   571\thandle's writer surface is on is part of the transport's declared nature: for a\n   572\tring buffer it is the logical stream itself, so `ok` places the value in it; for\n   573\ta transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere it is the ordered\n   574\toutbound path, and what becomes of the value at the far end is reported there,\n   575\tnot here. No remote transport can say more — under *no operation waits*, an\n   576\tanswer from the far end cannot arrive before `append!` returns.\n   577\t\n   578\tThe writer decides what to do about any non-`ok` outcome; DaoStream does not\n   579\tretry, buffer, or notify on its behalf.\n   654\t## Retention and Gaps\n   655\t\n   656\tRetention and overflow behavior are transport-owned and form part of the\n   657\tstream or writer surface's declared nature; a locally created stream records\n   658\tthem through its creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts\n   659\tthe oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send\n   660\tbuffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —\n   661\t`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or\n   662\tpermanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the\n   663\toutcomes and standardizes no policy.\n   664\t\n   665\t**Eviction never waits on a reader.** Retention is the stream's own declared\n   666\tpolicy and applies whatever any reader is doing — a stream holds no reader\n   667\tpositions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred\n   668\teviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\n   669\tabsence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\n   670\treader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\n   671\tdeliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a cursor that falls behind\n   672\treceives a `gap`.\n   673\t\n   674\tWhen bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n   675\tgap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\n   676\tnothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\n   677\ttelemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\n   678\tskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader holding a cursor across an\n   679\teviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\n   680\thandle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\n   681\tthat must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\n   682\t\n   683\t### Complete history\n   684\t\n   685\tSome consumers require *complete* history rather than *retained* history: an\n   686\tinterpreter that derives state by replaying a log from its beginning is wrong,\n   687\tnot merely stale, if it replays a suffix. The contract already carries what\n   688\tsuch a consumer needs, in two mechanisms that answer different questions.\n   689\t\n   690\t**A transport that excludes `gap` gives completeness.** This is the existing\n   691\tdeclaration in its existing form — the outcome's precondition is impossible by\n   692\tthe transport's nature, an unbounded log never evicts, so never reports `gap`\n   693\t(see *Surfaces*). Retention is declared through the transport's nature and, for\n   694\ta locally created stream, through its creation specification; it is\n   695\tconfiguration provenance. There is no way to ask a handle about it and none is\n   696\tadded. A consumer requiring complete history therefore requires *a transport of\n   697\tthat declared nature*, and the composition that wires it is what supplies one.\n   698\t\n   699\t**A kept origin cursor gives detection.** On a transport that can evict, an\n   700\torigin cursor turns a loss that would otherwise be invisible into a reported\n   701\t`gap`, and the consumer aborts or recovers on its own policy. It does not make\n   702\tthe history complete; it makes incompleteness observable.\n   703\t\n   704\tBoth stand, and they are not substitutes. Where completeness is a correctness\n   705\trequirement, only the declared transport delivers it, and a kept origin cursor\n   921\t**Proposed resolution.** Two decisions, the first a choice:\n   922\t\n   923\t1. **Choose what a logical identity ranges over.** Either\n   924\t   (a) *one origin, one transport*: a logical stream is the sequence one\n   925\t   transport instance owns. A copy on another host or medium is a different\n   926\t   logical stream with its own identity; that it mirrors the first is a fact for an\n   927\t   interpreter to record. This is how the code already behaves:\n   928\t   `dao.stream.forward` copies into a destination with its own identity. The\n   929\t   sentence\n   930\t   about different descriptors reaching one stream narrows to \"different\n   931\t   endpoints of the same owning transport\". Or\n   932\t   (b) *contract-level position*: the contract defines a position within an\n   933\t   identity (an ordinal), cursors carry it under a `:dao.stream/…` key, and any\n   934\t   transport serving that identity honors it.\n   935\t   Recommended: (a). It matches Axiom 2 (sameness between sequences is an\n   936\t   interpretation), costs a paragraph, and leaves (b) available later as an\n   937\t   additive key. (b) commits every future transport, including ones with no\n   938\t   natural ordinal, now.\n   939\t2. **Settle serialization.** Replace \"TBD\" with: \"A cursor is plain data and\n   940\t   survives the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, under the same\n   941\t   rule as the envelopes. A cursor that outlives the stream it names yields\n   942\t   `not-found` at `attach!` or `cursor-mismatch` at `next`, never a defect.\"\n   943\t   Both existing transports already satisfy this.\n   944\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"The revision removes relay-only frame names, but the meeting peer still provides routing and address observation as an application convention. I’m checking whether that convention has enough provenance, socket reuse, and loss handling to support the claimed mechanical NAT path."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md | sed -n '7,26p;32,51p;96,125p;336,371p;445,465p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md | sed -n '7,26p;32,51p;96,125p;336,371p;445,465p'\"","aggregated_output":"     7\t## What the Descriptor Names\n     8\t\n     9\tA WebSocket descriptor names a **server-hosted stream**, never a connection.\n    10\tThe stream exists on the serving host — created there, retained there,\n    11\tclosed there by its owner — and it exists whether or not any client is\n    12\tcurrently connected. A connection is the mechanics of one attachment:\n    13\tconnecting joins the stream, disconnecting detaches from it, and neither\n    14\tevent creates, closes, or destroys anything. That is what makes `attach!`\n    15\thonest here: the same descriptor reaches the same stream every time, because\n    16\tthe stream's identity and lifecycle belong to the server, not to the wire.\n    17\t\n    18\tThis framing generalizes: **serving is what makes any stream remotely\n    19\tattachable.** A ring buffer is host-local only because nothing speaks for\n    20\tit. A host that composes a WebSocket endpoint in front of one is serving\n    21\tthat ring buffer; the endpoint produces a descriptor carrying its address and\n    22\tthe ring buffer's unchanged logical-stream identity, and remote\n    23\tinterpreters can attach. The transport owns no stream — it serves streams\n    24\tthe host composed, which is exactly the contract's rule that descriptors\n    25\tresolve against \"the streams a network endpoint serves because serving them\n    26\tis what it is.\"\n    32\t## Surfaces\n    33\t\n    34\tA WebSocket handle is **writer** and **closable**, plus `descriptor` as every\n    35\thandle. It has no reader surface: a socket retains nothing, so it cannot make\n    36\tthe reader surface's promise — a positioned, append-only, retained sequence\n    37\tthat cursors can observe and re-observe. `cursor` and `next` do not exist on it.\n    38\t\n    39\tBoth a client handle and a server-side accepted-connection handle answer\n    40\t`descriptor` with reachability for the endpoint through which they attached\n    41\tand with the served stream's transport-independent `:dao.stream/identity`.\n    42\tTwo endpoints may therefore produce different descriptors for the same served\n    43\tstream, while every such result carries the same identity.\n    44\t`:dao.stream/attachment` distinguishes connections; neither the descriptor nor\n    45\tthe logical-stream identity does.\n    46\t\n    47\tThe writer surface is on the connection's **ordered outbound path** — the\n    48\tsequence of values accepted for sending on this attachment — never on the\n    49\tserved logical stream. `:dao.stream/ok` places the value in the outbound path;\n    50\twhat becomes of it at the far end is reported there, not here (contract,\n    51\tWriting).\n    96\t**Attachment identity.** This transport distinguishes its attachments, so its\n    97\t`attach!` success map carries `:dao.stream/attachment` as the contract\n    98\trequires: a descriptor names the served stream and cannot tell two attachments\n    99\tto it apart, and a handle is a host-local object that cannot be compared\n   100\tagainst data that crossed a codec.\n   101\t\n   102\tEvery event deposited for that attachment carries the same value under\n   103\t`:ws/attachment`. The key differs because the scopes differ: result keywords\n   104\tare the contract's and live under `:dao.stream/…`, while a deposited event is\n   105\ta stream element, where transport-owned keys live under the transport's\n   106\tnamespace. Correlation is by the value carried, never by the key's name.\n   107\t\n   108\tWithout that identity a shared medium is a multiplexed sequence with no\n   109\tdemultiplexing key: every event present, in order, and unattributable.\n   110\t\n   111\t**Server-minted attachment identity.** Before offering a server-side\n   112\taccepted-connection handle to the serving composition, the transport mints\n   113\tits attachment identity under the contract's server-minted-handle rule. The\n   114\tvalue is unique among attachments the boundary can distinguish and is not\n   115\treused during that boundary's lifetime.\n   116\t\n   117\tThe acceptance-offer event carries the value under `:ws/attachment`, and\n   118\tevery later deposited event for the connection carries the same value. The\n   119\toffer, its acknowledgement, the handle, and all later events therefore\n   120\tdenote one attachment. Client-side `attach!` continues to return the\n   121\tcorresponding client-local identity under `:dao.stream/attachment`.\n   122\t\n   123\t**Deposited events are envelopes**, so that payload and lifecycle are\n   124\tdistinguishable and both are attributable:\n   125\t\n   336\t### Ending a served stream\n   337\t\n   338\tClosing a served stream is its owner's `close!`, and connected clients have to\n   339\tbe able to tell that from a connection that merely dropped. The two call for\n   340\topposite responses — a dropped connection means reattach, an ended stream\n   341\tmeans stop — and a client has no reader surface, so the answer can only reach\n   342\tit as a deposited event.\n   343\t\n   344\tThe closing frame carries it. When the serving host closes a stream it closes\n   345\teach attached connection **with a distinguishing close code**, rather than\n   346\tdropping the socket. The client's adapter reads that code and deposits\n   347\t`:ws/ended` instead of `:ws/closed`. (The exact code is `4000`, mapping to\n   348\t`:ws/ended`, while `4002` is protocol-error and `4004` is the authoritative\n   349\tnot-found disclaimer; see Elements and Serialization.)\n   350\t\n   351\tWhat separates the two cases is not the closing but whether anything was\n   352\theard. A connection lost to a network fault delivers no closing frame at all,\n   353\tand the host reports that distinctly — the client learns that nobody told it\n   354\tanything, and deposits `:ws/closed`.\n   355\t\n   356\tThree outcomes follow, and the third is the honest limit:\n   357\t\n   358\t- **A closing frame arrived** — the stream ended. Stop; there is nothing to\n   359\t  reattach to.\n   360\t- **Nothing arrived** — reattach. If the stream is in fact gone, the handshake\n   361\t  answers with the authoritative disclaimer and the client learns on that\n   362\t  attempt instead of this one. A frame lost in transit costs one wasted\n   363\t  reattach, never a wrong conclusion the client cannot escape.\n   364\t- **The server cannot be reached at all** — unknowable. Nothing can distinguish\n   365\t  a crashed peer from an unreachable one, so a client keeps retrying, which is\n   366\t  correct: the stream may still be there. Bounding those retries is the\n   367\t  consumer's policy, not this transport's.\n   368\t\n   369\tThe close code is the fast path for the common case — a healthy server ending a\n   370\tstream deliberately. The handshake's disclaimer is what makes every other case\n   371\tresolve as soon as a server is reachable. Neither is sufficient alone.\n   445\t## Envelope\n   446\t\n   447\tTransport-owned keys are qualified under `:ws/…`; the logical-stream identity\n   448\tkey is contract-owned. The exact key set is settled by the descriptor gate;\n   449\tthese properties constrain it:\n   450\t\n   451\t- The descriptor is self-contained and exists to cross the network: it\n   452\t  carries what any host needs to reach the served stream — advertised host,\n   453\t  advertised port, canonical path, perhaps TLS parameters — plus the distinct\n   454\t  `:dao.stream/identity`. Endpoint address alone does not name a stream.\n   455\t- An endpoint constructor receives bind host/port and advertised host/port as\n   456\t  separate composition data. Bind defaults to `127.0.0.1`. An omitted\n   457\t  advertised host defaults to a concrete bind host, but a wildcard bind\n   458\t  requires an explicit advertised host. An omitted advertised port defaults to\n   459\t  the actual bound port, including the assigned port after a bind to zero.\n   460\t  Descriptors returned by accepted handles use the advertised values.\n   461\t- The descriptor carries reachability and logical-stream identity only. Where inbound traffic lands is the\n   462\t  host boundary's composition (see The Duplex Model) and appears in no\n   463\t  envelope and no argument of `attach!`.\n   464\t\n   465\t## The Handshake\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '272,291p;594,613p;631,650p;674,681p;1078,1093p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '272,291p;594,613p;631,650p;674,681p;1078,1093p'\"","aggregated_output":"   272\t- A transport whose attachments are distinguishable assigns each attachment\n   273\t  an opaque, structurally serializable value, unique among the attachments\n   274\t  that transport can tell apart. Every attachment handle the transport mints\n   275\t  has such an identity, whether the handle is returned by `attach!` or\n   276\t  minted from a host connection accepted without an `attach!` call. An\n   277\t  `attach!` success map carries the value under `:dao.stream/attachment`.\n   278\t  When no `attach!` call produces the handle, the transport specification\n   279\t  names the deposited event or other stream-native mechanism by which the\n   280\t  composition receives the identity. Wherever an answer or event for that\n   281\t  attachment is displaced onto another channel, the same value correlates it\n   282\t  there. The key and correlation rule are the contract's; minting,\n   283\t  representation, and the server-side delivery mechanism are the\n   284\t  transport's. Transports that cannot distinguish attachments omit it.\n   285\t- It carries no authorization. Nothing in this contract gates who may attach:\n   286\t  a descriptor is a name, and a host that can reach what it names attaches.\n   287\t  Whether attachment should be gated at all, and by what, is a question this\n   288\t  contract does not answer and does not reserve room for — an outcome or key\n   289\t  held open for an undesigned mechanism constrains that mechanism's design\n   290\t  from a document with no business specifying it. Should gating arrive, it\n   291\t  arrives as an addition, which the open-map rule and this section's own\n   594\t`close!` closes **what the handle is on**. A handle explicitly designated by\n   595\tthe transport as the logical stream's owner closes that logical stream; a\n   596\tserver-side accepted-connection handle is an attachment and does not gain\n   597\townership merely by being on the serving host. An attached\n   598\thandle is on an attachment: closing it ends that attachment only — the\n   599\tstream persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` may rejoin\n   600\tlater. Which scope a transport's handles carry is part of its declared\n   601\tnature.\n   602\t\n   603\t`:dao.stream/closed` and `:dao.stream/end` are handle-relative: they speak of\n   604\twhat *this handle* is on. (Not every outcome is: `gap` is retention-relative\n   605\tand `cursor-mismatch` is logical-stream-relative.) After an attachment\n   606\tcloses, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers\n   607\t`ok` with reachability and identity — both outlive any attachment; a reader surface, where the\n   608\thandle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this\n   609\thandle is exhausted, and `closed` from `cursor` — no new cursors are\n   610\tminted through a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream,\n   611\tnot to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.\n   612\t\n   613\tClosing an attachment freezes what that handle can still observe: values\n   631\t## Concurrency\n   632\t\n   633\tEach logical stream is one append-only sequence, and a handle's writer\n   634\tsurface is on exactly one sequence — for some transports the logical stream\n   635\titself, for others the ordered outbound path toward a stream elsewhere (see\n   636\tWriting). A successfully appended value occupies exactly one definite position\n   637\tin the sequence this handle's writer surface is on, and every operation's\n   638\toutcome reflects that sequence at one moment. DaoStream guarantees nothing\n   639\tfurther about concurrent operations.\n   640\t\n   641\tThis follows from Axiom 2. The sequence a handle's surfaces are on is the one\n   642\ttruth for that handle; each outcome is one perspective on it at one moment. A\n   643\treader that observes\n   644\t`:dao.stream/blocked` and then a value has not caught the stream in a\n   645\tcontradiction — it made two observations from its own cursor, and no global\n   646\tview exists to contradict. Whether an append landed before a close is\n   647\tanswered by the sequence the handle's writer surface is on — the value has a\n   648\tposition in it or it does not — and where that sequence is not observable from\n   649\tthis handle, the answer is read where it is.\n   650\tWhat an ordering between writers *means*, and any coordination among them —\n   674\tWhen bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n   675\tgap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\n   676\tnothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\n   677\ttelemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\n   678\tskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader holding a cursor across an\n   679\teviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\n   680\thandle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\n   681\tthat must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\n  1078\trules the contract already has, so none needs a decision now. They are listed\n  1079\tso that a later plan does not rediscover them as surprises.\n  1080\t\n  1081\t- **A writer does not learn where its value landed.** `append!`'s `ok` carries\n  1082\t  no cursor. Read-your-writes and causal references between streams arrive as\n  1083\t  an optional `:dao.stream/cursor` key on `ok`. Conditional append (\"only if\n  1084\t  the tail is still here\") would be an eighth operation; the design does not\n  1085\t  need it because it assumes **one writer per logical stream**, which\n  1086\t  `dao.space` practices and this document should say once in *Concurrency*.\n  1087\t- **History is not verifiable.** Identity is an opaque value nothing\n  1088\t  authenticates, and append-only is the transport's promise, not something a\n  1089\t  reader can check. Self-certifying identities and signed or hash-chained\n  1090\t  elements live in the payload, above the stream. The obligation on this layer\n  1091\t  is only the one it already meets: identity is compared structurally and never\n  1092\t  parsed.\n  1093\t- **Readiness.** Polling is O(n) in streams observed (*What it costs*). The\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '42,75p;129,162p;175,185p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '42,75p;129,162p;175,185p'\"","aggregated_output":"    42\t## 3. Can relay and rendezvous be ordinary peers? Yes, completely\n    43\t\n    44\tThe mechanism. Any peer M may choose to serve a **meeting stream pair** under identities it announces however it likes (a descriptor in a directory stream, a rendezvous topic in `dao.stream.discovery`, a URL on a wall). The pair is exactly the `dao.stream.ws.md` request-medium pattern, so nothing new is specified:\n    45\t\n    46\t- **`meet-requests`**: writer surface only. Any peer opens a session and appends requests. This is M's request medium; many writers, and the meaning of their order is M's interpreter's.\n    47\t- **`meet-announcements`**: reader surface only, single-writer (M). M's interpreter reads its request medium and appends facts.\n    48\t\n    49\tThree request kinds and three fact kinds, all convention vocabulary under `:meet/…`, none of them serve frames:\n    50\t\n    51\t- `{:meet/here <peer-id>}` → M creates two ring buffers, serves them, and announces `{:meet/inbox <peer-id> :dao.stream/identity <in> …}` with the serve descriptors of the pair. Any peer that reads the announcement stream now knows how to reach that peer through M.\n    52\t- On every request M's interpreter also announces `{:meet/seen <peer-id> :meet/reflexive {:host … :port …}}`, taken from the request's `:udp/attachment` in M's traffic medium. That is the whole of STUN: the reflexive address is a channel fact M already deposits, republished as a value.\n    53\t- `{:meet/call <peer-id> :meet/from <my-peer-id>}` → M announces it. Both parties read the announcement stream anyway, so both learn the other's reflexive address and that a call is in progress. That is the whole of the introduction.\n    54\t\n    55\t**Relayed channel.** A channel is a writer handle plus a reader handle (§6.1 revised). Peer A reaching B through M uses a proxy to B's inbox as its writer and a proxy to its own inbox as its reader. The serve protocol runs unchanged over it: sessions inside, `:serve/open` on B's serve table, cursors verbatim. M runs the ordinary serve-step over ring buffers and never sees a session it did not itself serve. Because the inner protocol already tolerates loss (idempotent reads, deduplicated appends), an inbox that evicts under pressure produces an inner `gap` on the outer proxy, which the inner retry policy absorbs.\n    56\t\n    57\t**Hole punching.** After a call is announced, both peers `attach!` a UDP channel to the other's reflexive address and send `:serve/ping`. The first inbound datagram from that address is deposited by the UDP adapter with that attachment identity; the composition matches it to the pending candidate and the direct channel exists. If no pong arrives in the punch budget, the inbox-through-M channel is the next candidate. No frame beyond `:serve/ping` and `:serve/pong` is involved.\n    58\t\n    59\t**What remains privileged:** nothing at the protocol level. Two facts remain, neither a privilege: M must have a reachable address (a network fact, and exactly why some peer has to be reachable for any two NATed peers to meet), and M must have chosen to run the meeting convention (a composition choice any peer can make). Under the reading in part 1 this satisfies the invariant. It is also stigmergy in the literal sense: peers coordinate through traces left on a shared medium, which is the project's founding model, and the meeting stream is the same object as the discovery document's directory stream.\n    60\t\n    61\tCosts, stated honestly: a relayed message is framed twice (outer session to M, inner session to the peer) and a peer must poll its inbox on M. The second cost is what changes Q2.\n    62\t\n    63\t## 4. Effect of the invariant on the Q1–Q7 consensus\n    64\t\n    65\t| Q | Consensus position | Effect | Revised position |\n    66\t|---|---|---|---|\n    67\t| Q1 | event-medium append; UCF `:put` resumes on observed source outcome; append-unknown leaves the wait undischarged | none; no role is involved | unchanged |\n    68\t| Q2 | defer held reads | **changes** | Include `:serve/hold` in v1, or at the latest in the same milestone as the meeting convention. The consensus deferred it when a relay was a forwarder that pushed frames. With no relay, a peer's reachability through M *is* polling its inbox proxy; held reads are what keep that from being a busy loop across the network. Deferring them now makes the only NAT fallback a chatty one. |\n    69\t| Q3 | opaque composition-assigned ids; duplicate `:serve/register` is relay policy; self-certifying later; public relay needs channel auth | **changes** | There is no assigner. A peer id is **minted by the peer itself**, from the start: a 128-bit random value at minimum, and in the self-certifying form (hash of the peer's public key, `dao.stream.discovery` mechanism 1) as the intended format, with signature verification an additive rule later. \"Composition-assigned\" must not mean assigned by a relay. Duplicate `:meet/here` for one id is an ordinary fact on M's announcement stream; what M's interpreter does with it is M's convention, and which meeting stream a reader trusts is the reader's choice, exactly as with directories. The \"public relay needs channel auth\" tripwire becomes \"a peer serving a public request stream gates it by an interpreter rule\", which the contract already permits. |\n    70\t| Q4 | relay admission is composition policy; lifecycle and caps required; postage future | **moves, substance survives** | There is no relay to admit. Inbox creation, retention and idle unserving are M's serve-table policy over its own request medium, identical to any served writer surface. Caps and lifecycle are required exactly as the consensus said, restated as \"M bounds the number and retention of inboxes it serves\". Postage stays the intended answer for a public meeting stream. |\n    71\t| Q5 | accept OD-2, OD-3, corrected OD-1; amend blocked and anchors | none | unchanged |\n    72\t| Q6 | lease-governed lifetime; retirement as stopgap | none; exporter and resumer are per-migration roles | unchanged |\n    73\t| Q7 | `dao.stream.serve`; rename `dao.stream.serving`; fix ws prose | strengthens | unchanged, plus: retitle §5, delete the relay and rendezvous sections, and rename the copy composition to say \"copy\" or \"broadcast\" so it reads as a convention. |\n    74\t\n    75\tThe consensus is not wrong where it stands; it was reached against a design that had a relay in it. Q2 and Q3 are the two places where removing the relay flips the answer.\n   129\t### §6.4 A channel over served streams (replaces \"A channel over a channel\")\n   130\t\n   131\tTwo proxied streams are a channel: peer A's writer is a proxy to a stream B reads, and A's reader is a proxy to a stream B writes. When a third peer M serves both streams, A and B have a channel without reaching each other, and M runs nothing but the ordinary serve-step over two ring buffers. Serve frames inside such a channel are values in M's streams and M never interprets them. This is streams as values sent through streams, and it is the whole of relaying.\n   132\t\n   133\t### §7 Reachability (replace §7.1 through §7.4)\n   134\t\n   135\t**7.1 The serve descriptor.** As before, with `:serve/peer` the holding peer's self-minted id, and `:serve/candidates` an ordered vector where each element is one of:\n   136\t\n   137\t- a **channel descriptor** (ws or udp) naming an acceptor address of the holding peer;\n   138\t- a **serve descriptor** for a stream some peer M serves that is the holding peer's inbox, together with the announcement-stream descriptor where M publishes the matching outbox: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/serve :dao.stream/identity <inbox> :serve/peer M :serve/meet <M's announcement-stream descriptor> :serve/candidates [M's channels]}`;\n   139\t- a **meeting resolution** `{:serve/meet <descriptor> :serve/peer <holding-peer-id>}`, meaning: attach to that announcement stream, append `:meet/call` to its request stream, read the holding peer's reflexive address, and attach a UDP channel to it while the holding peer does the same toward mine.\n   140\t\n   141\tThe proxy tries candidates in order; each attempt's resolution is deposited on the proxy's event medium. The descriptor carries no authorization.\n   142\t\n   143\t**7.2 Direct.** A peer with an acceptor channel, a listening ws endpoint or a bound UDP socket, is reached by a channel descriptor. This is establishment: the acceptor holds no authority the dialer lacks.\n   144\t\n   145\t**7.3 The meeting convention.** Any peer M may serve a **request stream** (writer surface; M's request medium, many writers) and an **announcement stream** (reader surface; single writer, M). An interpreter of M's composition reads the request medium and appends to the announcement stream. The vocabulary is a convention, not protocol:\n   146\t\n   147\t```clojure\n   148\t;; requests, appended by any peer through an ordinary session\n   149\t{:meet/here <peer-id>}                                   ; make me reachable through you\n   150\t{:meet/call <peer-id> :meet/from <peer-id>}              ; I want a channel to that peer\n   151\t\n   152\t;; facts, appended by M alone\n   153\t{:meet/inbox <peer-id> :serve/in <serve descriptor> :serve/out <serve descriptor>}\n   154\t{:meet/seen  <peer-id> :meet/reflexive {:host … :port …}}   ; from :udp/attachment on the request\n   155\t{:meet/call  <peer-id> :meet/from <peer-id>}                ; the call, republished\n   156\t```\n   157\t\n   158\tOn `:meet/here`, M creates two ring buffers, adds them to its serve table, and announces their descriptors; the named peer reads `in` and any peer may write it; the reverse for `out`. The pair, proxied, is a channel (§6.4). Retention, the number of pairs M keeps, and when an idle pair is unserved are M's composition policy, the same policy any served writer surface has. M is not a relay; it is a peer serving ring buffers, and a peer that reads M's announcements decides whether to trust them exactly as it decides whose directory to fold in `dao.stream.discovery`.\n   159\t\n   160\t**7.4 Punching.** After a call is announced, each party has the other's reflexive address from `:meet/seen`. Each `attach!`es a UDP channel to it and sends `:serve/ping` on the schedule its driver's `now` allows; the first inbound datagram from that address is deposited with that attachment identity, the composition matches it to the pending candidate, and a direct channel exists. No pong within the punch budget means the next candidate, normally the inbox pair on the same M. A symmetric NAT defeats punching and takes that path; the protocol reports a failed candidate, not a failure.\n   161\t\n   162\t**7.5 P2P symmetry** and **7.6 Liveness** unchanged, except that `:serve/ping` is also the punch probe.\n   175\t### §12 Deferred (replace the authorization item)\n   176\t\n   177\tGating of a public meeting request stream (postage or capability) is an interpreter rule on M's own medium; the contract gates nothing and this layer adds nothing.\n   178\t\n   179\t### §13 Completion criteria (replace items 5 and 6)\n   180\t\n   181\t5. **Reachability without acceptance.** A peer that only dials serves a stream to a peer that only dials, through an inbox pair on a third peer that runs nothing but `serve-step` and the meeting interpreter; the third peer's serve table holds two ring buffers and no other state about the session.\n   182\t6. **Punch and fallback.** Two UDP peers behind a NAT simulator obtain a direct channel through a meeting stream's `:meet/seen` facts and `:serve/ping`; under a symmetric-NAT simulator they fall to the inbox pair, reported as a failed candidate on the proxy's event medium.\n   183\t\n   184\tAdd: 10. **Symmetry proof.** The same peer composition, with no configuration difference beyond which channels it has, plays the accepted-side, dialed-side, inbox-holding and inbox-using positions in items 5 and 6.\n   185\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '49,105p;107,131p;188,201p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '49,105p;107,131p;188,201p'\"","aggregated_output":"    49\t### 3.1 Sessions\n    50\t\n    51\tA **session** is one attachment to one served logical stream over one channel. It is the serve-layer unit that carries `:dao.stream/attachment`. One channel carries many sessions, tagged by `:serve/session`, minted by the opening side and unique per channel. Multiplexing is here rather than in the channel so that channels stay dumb and so that one channel between two peers carries every stream a migrated continuation references.\n    52\t\n    53\tRoles are per session, not per channel: on one channel, peer A may serve stream X to B while B serves stream Y to A. This is the P2P case and needs no extra mechanism.\n    54\t\n    55\t### 3.2 Frames\n    56\t\n    57\tServe frames are the application values a channel carries. Keys are qualified under `:serve/…` with one separator, per the ws rule. The operation and its arguments ride in the `dao.stream.apply` envelope, which already owns request identity, operation and arguments:\n    58\t\n    59\t```clojure\n    60\t;; session control\n    61\t{:serve/frame :serve/open     :serve/session s  :dao.stream/identity id}\n    62\t{:serve/frame :serve/opened   :serve/session s  :dao.stream/attachment a\n    63\t :dao.stream/surface #{:reader :writer :closable}\n    64\t :serve/oldest <cursor>  :serve/newest <cursor>}                       ; anchors as of open, §3.6\n    65\t{:serve/frame :serve/disclaim :serve/session s}                        ; the authoritative not-found\n    66\t{:serve/frame :serve/close    :serve/session s}                        ; either direction, terminal\n    67\t\n    68\t;; operations\n    69\t{:serve/frame :serve/request  :serve/session s\n    70\t :dao.stream.apply/id n  :dao.stream.apply/op :dao.stream/next  :dao.stream.apply/args [cursor budget]}\n    71\t{:serve/frame :serve/response :serve/session s\n    72\t :dao.stream.apply/id n  :dao.stream.apply/ok <see 3.3>\n    73\t :serve/oldest <cursor>  :serve/newest <cursor>}                       ; piggybacked refresh, §3.6\n    74\t\n    75\t;; channel liveness (per channel, not per session)\n    76\t{:serve/frame :serve/ping :serve/nonce k}\n    77\t{:serve/frame :serve/pong :serve/nonce k}\n    78\t\n    79\t;; reachability control, §7\n    80\t{:serve/frame :serve/register  :serve/peer p}                          ; outbound-serving host → relay or rendezvous\n    81\t{:serve/frame :serve/dial      :serve/peer p  :serve/link l}           ; client → relay/rendezvous: reach p\n    82\t{:serve/frame :serve/introduce :serve/peer q  :serve/link l  :serve/reflexive {…}}   ; rendezvous → p\n    83\t{:serve/frame :serve/via       :serve/link l  :serve/inner <frame>}    ; relayed carriage, §6.4\n    84\t{:serve/frame :serve/punch     :serve/link l  :serve/nonce k}          ; hole-punch probe, §7.4\n    85\t```\n    86\t\n    87\tA frame with an unknown `:serve/frame`, a missing required key, or a value outside the channel's portable domain is a protocol failure of the **channel** (decode diagnostic then teardown), never a serve outcome: the serve layer only ever sees valid frames, exactly as the ws adapter only deposits valid events.\n    88\t\n    89\t### 3.3 Mechanical derivation\n    90\t\n    91\tThe server for a handle `h` with declared surface `S` is the apply handler table below, restricted to `S`. Nothing about `h`'s transport appears anywhere; a string-backed stream, a ring buffer and a durable log are served by the same six lines.\n    92\t\n    93\t| Wire op                | Args                     | Server computes                          | `:dao.stream.apply/ok` value                              |\n    94\t|------------------------|--------------------------|------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|\n    95\t| `:dao.stream/descriptor` | `[]`                   | `(descriptor h)`                         | the outcome map, verbatim                                 |\n    96\t| `:dao.stream/cursor`   | `[anchor]`               | `(cursor h anchor)`                      | the outcome map, verbatim                                 |\n    97\t| `:dao.stream/next`     | `[cursor budget]`        | `next` repeated from `cursor`, following each `ok`'s successor, at most `budget` times, stopping at the first non-`ok` | a vector of outcome maps, in order, verbatim |\n    98\t| `:dao.stream/append!`  | `[value]`                | `(append! h value)`                      | the outcome map, verbatim                                 |\n    99\t| `:dao.stream/close!`   | `[]`                     | `(close! h)` only if `h` is an attachment the server minted for this session; never the owner's close | `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}` |\n   100\t\n   101\tAn op absent from `S` is answered with an apply **error** response, code `:dao.stream.serve/no-surface`: the contract forbids answering a nonexistent operation with an outcome map (Surfaces), and the apply envelope has an error shape for exactly this. The proxy never exposes such an op, so the error only ever reaches a misbehaving client.\n   102\t\n   103\tOutcome maps cross **verbatim**, including the cursors inside them. The serve layer never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor. This is the whole basis of cursor-namespace preservation: the proxy hands the reader exactly the cursor the source minted, and hands the source exactly the cursor the reader held.\n   104\t\n   105\t**Precondition on the source.** Its cursors and outcome maps must lie in the channel's portable value domain. Both existing transports qualify (a string identity and an integer position). A transport whose cursor holds a host object cannot be served; the exporter refuses it before publishing (§9). This is the whole content of the UCF's \"portable cursor profile\": one profile, `:dao.stream.serve/v1`, meaning *cursor is plain data in the portable domain and the source honors it after serialization*.\n   107\t### 3.4 Idempotence, loss and duplication\n   108\t\n   109\tFour of the five operations are idempotent as the contract defines them: `descriptor` and `cursor` are projections; `next` is non-destructive and positioned by its argument; `close!` is idempotent by definition. A lost request is re-sent; a lost response is re-requested; a duplicated request re-computes the same answer or a later, equally true one. **No sequence numbers, acknowledgement vectors or sliding windows are needed for reads: the cursor is the sequence number, and `gap` remains the source's declaration rather than a transport artifact.** This is what reconciles UDP with kept cursors and gap, and why v1 DRDS (`daostream-udp-design.md`) is not revived.\n   110\t\n   111\t`append!` is the one non-idempotent operation. The protocol makes it safe by the rule OD-2 already states — deduplication and correlation are the payload's — applied at the serve layer so that no application has to:\n   112\t\n   113\t- A session has **at most one outstanding `append!`**. The proxy does not issue the next until the previous is answered or the session is lost. This is `dao.stream.rpc`'s `unsent` discipline, and it also makes UDP reordering harmless for writes.\n   114\t- The server keeps, per session, the id and outcome of the **last** `append!` it performed. A request with that id is answered from the record without re-appending. Window size one follows from the one-outstanding rule.\n   115\t- If the session is lost with an append unanswered, the proxy reports it as `:dao.stream.serve/append-unknown` on its event medium with the retained value. The proxy does not silently retry across sessions: the far end may have appended. This is OD-2's third state named honestly.\n   116\t\n   117\tA stale response (an id no longer outstanding) is a diagnostic, not an error, exactly as in `dao.stream.rpc`.\n   118\t\n   119\t### 3.5 Batching\n   120\t\n   121\t`next`'s `budget` bounds one response. The server answers with consecutive outcomes; the proxy installs each `(cursor_i → outcome_i)` with `cursor_0` the requested cursor and `cursor_{i+1}` the successor in `outcome_i`. A server also bounds the batch by the channel's frame budget (§6.3): it stops early when the next value would not fit and returns what it has. A single value that cannot fit in one frame on this channel is answered as `transport-error` with `:dao.stream.serve/reason :oversize` in place of that element; the read is not silently skipped and the cursor does not advance past it. Large media belong in the payload as `dao.jing` addresses (contract, Granularity).\n   122\t\n   123\t### 3.6 Anchors across a hop\n   124\t\n   125\t`cursor` has no `blocked` outcome, yet a proxy cannot consult the source synchronously. The design does not invent a proxy-namespaced cursor (that would put a cursor in circulation that the source's own handle rejects, violating \"any handle on the same logical stream accepts it\"). Instead the server **piggybacks fresh `:oldest` and `:newest` cursors on `:serve/opened` and on every response**, and the proxy's `cursor` answers with the most recently observed one.\n   126\t\n   127\tThis is honest under the contract's own definitions. A stale `:newest` is *earlier* than a fresh one, so a composition that mints `:newest` before invoking an operation still observes everything the operation causes; it may additionally observe a few values appended between the last exchange and the mint, which is re-seeing, never skipping. A stale `:oldest` may point at a position since evicted, and the contract already says a fresh `:oldest` is a position rather than a completeness claim: the reader receives `gap` with the recovery cursor. Before the first `:serve/opened` the proxy holds no anchors and `cursor` answers `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true` (OD-1's proposed key); the composition observes `:serve/opened` on the proxy's event medium and mints then.\n   128\t\n   129\t### 3.7 Held reads (optional, v1 decision needed)\n   130\t\n   131\tAcross a network a `blocked` answer costs a round trip. A `next` request may carry `:serve/hold true`; a server that honors it records the demand in session state and, on each of its own driver steps, re-polls the source, answering when the outcome is no longer `blocked` or when its hold budget (a constructor policy in the driver's clock domain) elapses, in which case it answers the `blocked` it has. No operation waits: the session holds state, the step returns. A proxy must tolerate a server that ignores the flag. Whether v1 includes this is an owner decision (Q2).\n   188\t### 6.3 UDP channel\n   189\t\n   190\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the ws deposit model and no reader surface: a socket retains nothing. It is the datagram boundary the DHT node already uses, reshaped into the adapter form.\n   191\t\n   192\t- **Descriptor.** `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity <peer-id> :udp/host … :udp/port …}`. `attach!` binds a local socket (ephemeral port) and returns a handle whose writer path is \"datagrams to that address\". No handshake is needed at this layer; the serve layer's `:serve/open` is the handshake.\n   193\t- **One value per datagram**, encoded with the CBOR codec profile (binary, canonical, already specified in ws). Frame budget 1200 bytes, the DHT's figure, chosen to clear common path MTUs without fragmentation. A value over budget is `invalid-value` on `append!` and, from the server's side, `:oversize` (§3.5). IP fragmentation is not relied on; application fragmentation is deferred and, if added, lives in this channel, invisible to the serve layer.\n   194\t- **Declared nature:** `:unordered`, `:best-effort`. The proxy's retry policy therefore has real work: each outstanding request records `:sent-at`; `proxy-step` re-sends when `now - sent-at` exceeds `:retry-after-ms`, with `:max-attempts`, both composition data. Exhaustion is session loss, deposited as data.\n   195\t- **Lifecycle events** are thinner than ws: there is no peer close, so `:udp/closed` is deposited only on local close or deposit failure; peer silence is the serve layer's business (`:serve/ping` and the retry policy), not the channel's.\n   196\t- **Attachment identity** is the remote `(host, port)` pair as seen by the socket; replies go to the datagram's source address, never to a claimed address in the payload, as the DHT already rules.\n   197\t- Hosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs node (`dgram`), cljd (`RawDatagramSocket`). **Browsers have no UDP.** The browser's P2P path is a WebSocket to a relay (§7.3) today and a WebRTC DataChannel channel later (§14); both slot into §6.1 without touching the serve layer.\n   198\t\n   199\t### 6.4 A channel over a channel\n   200\t\n   201\tA relayed path is a channel whose writer wraps every value as `{:serve/frame :serve/via :serve/link l :serve/inner v}` and whose adapter unwraps `:serve/via` frames with its link from the underlying channel's medium, depositing `:serve/inner`. It declares the underlying channel's ordering and reliability. Nothing else is new: streams are values that travel through streams, and a channel is a stream. The relay itself (§7.3) sees only `:serve/via` and a link table.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
