{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '202,217p;257,271p;319,326p;387,401p;421,432p'\"","aggregated_output":"   202\t## Creation and Attachment\n   203\t\n   204\tCreation brings a new logical stream into existence. Attachment joins an\n   205\texisting one. They are separate operations with separate inputs.\n   206\t\n   207\t- `create!` consumes a creation specification and returns a handle on a\n   208\t  new logical stream. `descriptor` on that handle returns `{ok}` with both\n   209\t  `:dao.stream/descriptor`, the transport-specific reachability value, and\n   210\t  `:dao.stream/identity`, the transport-independent logical-stream identity.\n   211\t  The descriptor carries the same identity internally; the sibling projection\n   212\t  and its value inside `:dao.stream/descriptor` must be structurally equal.\n   213\t  Both projections are total and outlive any attachment, so `descriptor` has\n   214\t  no failure outcome.\n   215\t- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on an\n   216\t  attachment to the **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching\n   217\t  never creates.\n   257\tThe **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object\n   258\treference. It is reachability data, not the logical-stream identity itself.\n   259\tIts properties are settled:\n   260\t\n   261\t- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch\n   262\t  key.\n   263\t- It carries the logical-stream identity under the contract-owned\n   264\t  `:dao.stream/identity` key. Different descriptors may reach\n   265\t  the same logical stream through different endpoints; their reachability data\n   266\t  may differ, but their logical-stream identity is structurally equal. The same\n   267\t  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.\n   268\t- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is\n   269\t  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is\n   270\t  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns\n   271\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.\n   319\t`attach!` outcomes:\n   320\t\n   321\t| Outcome                          | Meaning                                                                             | Required keys        |\n   322\t|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n   323\t| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | A handle exists through which this attachment is operated. Implies neither that a far end has confirmed the stream it names nor that establishment has completed. Where either answer is one only the far end can give, it arrives later as data, on the channel the transport names (see Surfaces). | `:dao.stream/handle`, and `:dao.stream/attachment` where the transport distinguishes attachments |\n   324\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |\n   325\t| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |\n   326\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |\n   387\t## Surfaces\n   388\t\n   389\tA handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can\n   390\thonor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**\n   391\t(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and\n   392\tRetention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.\n   393\t\n   394\t`descriptor` belongs to no surface: every handle answers it with both\n   395\treachability and logical-stream identity, because both outlive any attachment.\n   396\tAn operation a handle does not\n   397\tdeclare is not part of that handle at all: there is nothing to call, so there\n   398\tis no operation for this contract to give an outcome. What a host does when\n   399\tasked for a method that is not there is the host's, and no transport may\n   400\tanswer such a call with a DaoStream outcome map — an outcome would imply the\n   401\toperation exists.\n   421\tThe reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as\n   422\tone positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\n   423\tre-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that\n   424\tpromise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the\n   425\thost's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary\n   426\t— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a\n   427\ttransport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the\n   428\ttransport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per\n   429\tits declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly\n   430\tdefect. Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated: the contract's\n   431\tpublic surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy. A constructor\n   432\ttakes the deposit destination and its admission declaration as data. This is\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"agent_message","text":"The main blockers are concrete: the request value does not carry the UDP attachment that the meeting interpreter is told to republish; the revised candidate omits part of the request/announcement path; and opening a new UDP attachment may use a different local socket from the one the meeting peer observed. I’m folding these into precise replacement text."}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-25 13:05:29 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 20:05:29 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n## Mechanism\n\n**The meeting convention is permitted by the owner’s rulings, but the revised NAT path is not yet mechanically derived from DaoStream alone.** Every peer can run the same serve protocol, and a reachable peer may choose to host meeting streams. That removes a privileged *protocol node kind*. The meeting peer still performs routing, address observation, allocation, and fanout as a composition with costs and policy that must be specified. [revision:42](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:42), [revision:55](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:55)\n\nFour gaps prevent the claimed mechanical result:\n\n- **Surfaces and provenance.** The serving rule exposes the supplied handle’s declared surface. The meeting interpreter needs private read access to requests and private write access to announcements, while visitors get the opposite surfaces; that requires explicit restricted handles. More seriously, a `:meet/here` value in the request medium does not contain the UDP attachment identity that §3 says M republishes as `:meet/seen`. DaoStream’s attachment correlation is delivered to the composition, not automatically inserted into appended values. [original design:91](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:91), [revision:46](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:46), [revision:52](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:52), [dao.stream.md:272](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:272)\n- **UDP punching.** The observed reflexive address must belong to the *same bound UDP socket* used for probes. The original UDP `attach!` binds an ephemeral socket; a fresh attachment to the other peer can use another port. A matching nonce-bearing pong, rather than the first inbound datagram, must establish bidirectional reachability. [original design:192](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:192), [revision:160](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:160)\n- **Nested loss and size.** Two proxied streams can carry inner serve frames, but an outer inbox `gap` is loss of *channel frames*, not a `gap` in the final served stream. The inner driver needs a bounded recovery rule for lost or reordered control frames and append-unknown across sessions. Double framing also reduces the UDP payload budget; the existing design refuses an oversized single value rather than making every stream value transferable. [revision:55](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:55), [original design:113](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:113), [original design:121](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:121), [original design:193](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:193)\n- **Fairness.** One accepted `:meet/here` can allocate two buffers, while each request can create an announcement read by many peers. Inbox caps alone do not bound request processing, announcement retention, or fanout. A bounded request budget, admission decision, and observable refusal or timeout are needed. A lagging announcement reader can receive `gap` and miss a call. [revision:49](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:49), [revision:145](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:145), [dao.stream.md:674](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:674)\n\n## Changed questions\n\n- **Q2 — retain the earlier consensus: defer held reads.** Inbox polling adds traffic, but the existing retry interval and attempt budget already prevent a busy loop. Holds trade network traffic for per-session state and repeated polls at M; they are an optional performance choice, not a condition for NAT reachability. If added, bound held demands and work per driver step. [revision:68](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:68), [original design:131](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:131), [original design:194](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:194)\n- **Q3 — retain the earlier consensus, clarified:** the peer’s **own composition** mints its opaque random ID; no meeting peer assigns it. A public-key hash without proof of control provides no authenticated peer binding. Discovery’s kickoff-hash rule concerns stream identity and verifies signatures; it is not an existing peer-registration rule. Define duplicate `:meet/here` handling in the meeting convention. [revision:69](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:69), [dao.stream.discovery.md:70](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:70), [dao.stream.md:1087](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:1087)\n- **Q4 — agree that policy moves to M’s composition; retain the earlier policy’s full scope.** Bound request admission and work, announcements, inbox count and bytes, and idle lifetime. Open admission belongs to explicitly trusted deployments; postage remains a possible public policy. Calling M an ordinary peer does not remove the resources it allocates. [revision:70](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:70), [dao.stream.discovery.md:159](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:159)\n\n**Q1, Q5, and Q7 keep their decisions**, but Q1 must correlate both hops’ append outcomes, Q5 must distinguish an outer inbox gap from a final source gap, and Q7 needs the wider prose correction. **Q6 is operationally affected:** a source’s served-entry lifetime is insufficient if M retires the inbox pair while a migrated task still needs that route. [revision:55](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:55), [revision:158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:158)\n\n## Replacement wording for the revision\n\n- **§6.2 identity:** “A WebSocket descriptor’s `:dao.stream/identity` names the logical stream exposed at that endpoint. The acceptor’s peer ID is separate reachability data under `:serve/peer`; dialing or accepting confers no stream ownership.” A peer ID cannot silently replace the contract’s logical-stream identity. [revision:127](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:127), [dao.stream.md:263](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:263)\n- **§7.3 meeting streams:** “M owns the request and announcement media. It serves restricted writer-only and reader-only handles respectively, retaining its own complementary handles. A meeting request has a correlation ID. M admits it under a bounded policy, records its outcome as an announcement, and attributes an observed UDP address only through an explicit association between the request and the UDP channel event; a bare peer-ID claim supplies no address or authority.” [revision:145](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:145), [dao.stream.md:389](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:389)\n- **§6.4 and §7.4:** “An outer inbox gap is channel loss. Advance its outer cursor to the returned recovery cursor and recover outstanding inner requests under their existing IDs; never report that gap as eviction from the final served stream. A punch candidate reuses the UDP socket whose reflexive address M observed. It becomes direct only after a matching nonce-bearing pong confirms the path; otherwise the bounded attempt fails and the inbox path remains available.” [revision:131](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:131), [revision:160](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:160)\n- **§13 criterion 10:** “The same serve and proxy implementation supports every peer; a peer may additionally choose the meeting interpreter and its explicit resource policy. Tests cover two dial-only peers, loss and reordering at both framing layers, bounded meeting work, announcement gaps, and fallback.” This matches the owner’s allowance for a convention without pretending every composition has identical configuration. [revision:184](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:184)"}}
